A||||4||86 GT||How to remove the inner tie rod ?||||5||
Z||000000||86 GT||10-30-2000||09:25 AM||||I tried this past weekend to install a set of new inner tie rods, however I could not remove the factory original inner tie rod from the steering rack. The originals have a bevel on each side near the bushing as does the end of the steering rack, to allow you to fit a wrench on them. The problem is the inner tie rod seems to have been pressed onto the steering rack, the flat sides of the bushing have been compressed after it was screwed onto the steering rack. Any help would be great.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||10-30-2000||11:31 AM||||What kind of auto parts stores do you have up there in Canada?<BR>Do any of them rent tools? You need 2 'special' tools to accomplish the job. I can rent them here from the local Auto Zone.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||10-30-2000||01:46 PM||||The flat sides of the "Bushing" are what they call staked(flattened into a recess). Don't use a wrench on those flats  put wrenches outside of those flats and turn, the staking will break loose and the whole shebang will unscrew.||||reg||1
Z||000003||prof bobo||10-30-2000||03:42 PM||||The inner tie rods (sometimes called 'sockets' for some reason) are threaded onto the steering rack and then the outer sleeve is staked down to the flats of the nut to keep it from ever working loose.  To remove the tie rod (yep, the whole shebang), you put a wrench on the flats where it's been staked, another wrench across the rack gear (the right size wrench will hold it and not cause damage, you could probably put it in a vise) and unscrew the tie rod, unscrewing it will un-stake it.  Be sure to save the plastic nylon bushing(s) as you'll have to reuse it it the new rods didn't include them.  Screw the new rods on and stake them with a drift or chisel (I use a hydraulic press).  You can use hose clamps on the boot.  Hope that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000004||PJ||10-30-2000||07:57 PM||||Big wrenches are expensive so I use a pipe wrench to undo the things. A lot of the ones I've taken apart had threadlocker on the threads. Use a small propane torch to break the Loctite bond and make disassembly easier.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<P>||||reg||1
